=====Further reading=====
* {{pedia}}

===Etymology 2===
{{inh+|en|enm|libbe}}, from {{inh|en|ang|lybb}}, {{m|ang|lyb||medicine, drug, potion, poison, charm}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*lubi}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*lubją||wort, herb, drug, poison}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*lewbʰ-}}, {{m|ine-pro|*lewb-||to peel, break, damage}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*lew-||to cut, remove, prune, separate}}. Cognate with {{cog|de|Luppe}}, {{m|de|Lüppe||salve, ointment, plant juice, medicine, magic}}, {{cog|is|lyf||medicine, drug}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|UK|_|dialectal|Scotland}} A [[potion]]; [[magic]] [[potion]]; [[charm]]; [[concoction]].

===Etymology 3===
From {{der|en|enm|*libben}} (suggested by {{m|enm|libbyng|t=gelding}}, {{m|enm|lybbere|t=gelder}}), related to {{cog|nl|-}} {{m|dum|lubben||to castrate, emasculate}}, {{cog|nl|libbe||a steer}}, {{m|nl|lubbert||a eunuch}}. Further relation uncertain. Possibly related to {{cog|ang|*lybban||to doctor}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*lubbjan}}; or perhaps related to {{cog|ang|lappa}}, {{m|ang|læppa||lappet, piece, section, lobe, portion, district}}. More at {{l|en|lop}}.

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive|UK|_|dialectal|Northern England|Scotland}} To [[geld]]; [[castrate]]; [[emasculate]] (usually said of animals).

